<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ordinance of Villers-Cotterêts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1539</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ordinance of Villers-Cotterêts was a reforming law issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in August 1539 at his château of Villers-Cotterêts. Among its many articles on justice and administration, it required that legal and official acts be written in French rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and it ordered parish priests to keep registers of baptisms. It is the oldest French law still partly in force.</p></div>
